# 15. SMART TEACHING AND ADAPTIVE LEARNING

Create an intelligent learning engine.

Do not give every student the same questions in the same order.

Track learning performance.

If a student struggles with offer and acceptance, identify the weakness and provide simpler explanations, fresh scenarios and further practice.

If a student demonstrates mastery, introduce more complex problems.

Use:
- Spaced repetition.
- Active recall.
- Retrieval practice.
- Progressive difficulty.
- Worked examples.
- Immediate feedback.
- Interleaved practice.
- Reflection and self-explanation.

After every mistake, teach.

Do not merely display "Wrong Answer."

Explain the mistake, show the relevant principle, provide authority and offer another opportunity to solve a similar problem.

# 16. SOURCE ACCURACY AND LEGAL SAFETY

This is NON-NEGOTIABLE.

Never invent:
- Court cases.
- Case citations.
- Statutes.
- Constitutional provisions.
- Judicial quotations.
- Legal principles.
- Court decisions.
- Legal authorities.

Every legal teaching item must be traceable to a source.

Every item must have:
- Jurisdiction.
- Source name.
- Source link.
- Document title.
- Citation, where applicable.
- Relevant page, paragraph or section.
- Date of publication or judgment, where known.
- Date last verified.
- Verification status.
- Content reuse status.

Use primary legal authority wherever possible.

If two sources conflict, flag the conflict and prioritize verified authoritative material.

Differentiate current law from repealed, amended, historical or uncertain law.

Never assume an old statutory provision remains in force.

Clearly indicate when legal materials have not been independently checked.

For AI-generated educational explanations, link back to the source material.

Implement a reviewer workflow so a qualified Ghanaian legal educator or lawyer can inspect and approve content before it is published as verified.

This is an educational product, not a substitute for a lawyer or formal legal advice.

